ssive deformity • Guided growth/hemiepiphysiodesis in growing children • Corrective osteotomy in mature patients • Knee replacement for severe degenerative disease --- **6 GENU VALGUM** **DEFINITION** Genu valgum or knock knee deformity is a condition in which the knees approximate or touch while the ankles remain separated **CAUSES** • Physiological childhood valgus • Rickets • Obesity • Growth plate injury • Malunion • Skeletal dysplasia • Osteoarthritis • Previous trauma **CLINICAL FEATURES** • Knees come together • Ankles remain apart • Altered gait • Patellar maltracking • Knee pain • Difficulty running/walking in severe cases **COMPLICATIONS** • Patellofemoral instability • Lateral compartment overload • Early osteoarthritis • Gait abnormalities • Functional limitation **CONSERVATIVE MANAGEMENT** • Treatment of underlying cause • Weight management • Hip and knee strengthening • Gait training • Orthoses in selected cases **SURGICAL MANAGEMENT** • Guided growth • Distal femoral osteotomy • Corrective osteotomy • Joint replacement in severe degenerative disease --- **7 GENU RECURVATUM** **DEFINITION** Genu recurvatum is a deformity in which the knee is excessively extended beyond the normal physiological range **CAUSES** • Ligamentous laxity • Neuromuscular disorders • Poliomyelitis • Quadriceps weakness • Anterior capsular laxity • Trauma • Malunion • Abnormal tibial slope • Poor gait mechanics **CLINICAL FEATURES** • Hyperextension of knee • Posterior displacement of knee during standing • Knee pain • Instability • Abnormal gait • Quadriceps weakness • Difficulty controlling knee during stance **COMPLICATIONS** • Chronic ligament strain • Knee instability • Pain • Degenerative changes • Meniscal injury • Gait dysfunction **CONSERVATIVE MANAGEMENT** • Quadriceps and hamstring strengthening • Proprioceptive training • Gait retraining • Knee brace/orthosis • Correction of underlying neuromuscular disorder • Activity modification **SURGICAL MANAGEMENT** • Ligament reconstruction where indicated • Corrective osteotomy • Growth modulation in selected children • Other reconstruction based on structural cause --- **8 COXA VARA** **DEFINITION** Coxa vara is a deformity in which the femoral neck shaft angle is reduced generally below approximately 120 in adults **CAUSES** • Congenital/developmental conditions • Rickets • Trauma • Fracture malunion • Fibrous dysplasia • Perthes disease • Metabolic bone disease **CLINICAL FEATURES** • Shortening of limb • Limp • Trendelenburg gait • Reduced hip abduction • Apparent shortening • Hip pain • Muscle weakness **COMPLICATIONS** • Progressive deformity • Hip instability • Early osteoarthritis • Limb length discrepancy • Abductor weakness **RADIOLOGY** • Reduced neck shaft angle • Shortened/vertical femoral neck • Altered femoral head mechanics **CONSERVATIVE MANAGEMENT** • Treat underlying metabolic disorder • Physiotherapy • Hip abductor strengthening • Gait training • Activity modification **SURGICAL MANAGEMENT** • Corrective valgus osteotomy • Internal fixation • Treatment of underlying bone pathology --- **9 COXA VALGA** **DEFINITION** Coxa valga is a deformity in which the femoral neck shaft angle is increased generally above approximately 135 in adults **CAUSES** • Neuromuscular disorders • Developmental abnormalities • Hip dysplasia • Cerebral palsy • Muscle imbalance • Certain metabolic/skeletal disorders **CLINICAL FEATURES** • Reduced hip stability • Limp • Abductor weakness • Limited hip mechanics • Possible hip pain • Increased tendency toward subluxation in susceptible patients **COMPLICATIONS** • Hip instability • Subluxation/dislocation • Abductor insufficiency • Altered gait • Early degenerative changes **MANAGEMENT** • Treat underlying condition • Hip strengthening • Gait training • Orthotic management when indicated • Corrective femoral osteotomy in significant symptomatic deformity • Hip reconstruction when associated with instability/dysplasia --- **10 PES CAVUS** **DEFINITION** Pes cavus is a foot deformity characterized by an abnormally high medial longitudinal arch often associated with plantarflexion of the first ray hindfoot varus and clawing of the toes **CAUSES** • Neuromuscular disorders • Hereditary motor sensory neuropathy • Poliomyelitis • Cerebral palsy • Spinal cord disorders • Trauma • Idiopathic causes **CLINICAL FEATURES** • High medial arch • Shortened foot appearance • Hindfoot varus • Claw toes • Prominent metatarsal heads • Lateral border weight bearing • Callosities • Foot pain • Ankle instability **COMPLICATIONS** • Metatarsalgia • Calluses • Ankle sprains • Plantar fascia pain • Difficulty wearing shoes • Progressive deformity • Gait abnormality **CONSERVATIVE MANAGEMENT** • Treat underlying neurological disease • Custom orthoses • Appropriate footwear • Stretching • Strengthening • Balance training • Gait correction • Pressure redistribution **SURGICAL MANAGEMENT** • Plantar fascia release in selected flexible deformities • Tendon transfer • Calcaneal osteotomy • First ray osteotomy • Midfoot osteotomy • Arthrodesis for rigid severe deformity --- **11 PES PLANUS** **DEFINITION** Pes planus or flat foot is a condition in which the medial longitudinal arch of the foot is reduced or absent often accompanied by excessive hindfoot valgus and pronation **CAUSES** • Ligamentous laxity • Posterior tibial tendon dysfunction • Obesity • Trauma • Rheumatoid arthritis • Neuromuscular disorders • Degenerative changes • Tarsal coalition **CLINICAL FEATURES** • Flattened medial arch • Excessive pronation • Hindfoot valgus • Heel eversion • Forefoot abduction • Foot fatigue • Medial foot/ankle pain • Difficulty prolonged standing/walking • Abnormal shoe wear **COMPLICATIONS** • Posterior tibial tendon dysfunction • Plantar fasciitis • Ankle pain • Knee/hip biomechanical problems • Progressive deformity • Difficulty walking **CONSERVATIVE MANAGEMENT** • Weight management • Footwear modification • Arch support orthoses • Heel support • Stretching of tight calf muscles • Foot intrinsic strengthening • Posterior tibial muscle strengthening • Balance and gait training • Activity modification **SURGICAL MANAGEMENT** For symptomatic rigid/progressive deformity • Tendon reconstruction • Calcaneal osteotomy • Midfoot reconstruction • Arthrodesis for rigid deformity • Procedures addressing the underlying cause --- **12 HALLUX RIGIDUS** **DEFINITION** Hallux rigidus is a degenerative arthritic condition of the first metatarsophalangeal joint characterized predominantly by painful restriction of great toe dorsiflexion **CAUSES** • Degenerative osteoarthritis • Previous trauma • Repetitive loading • Anatomical abnormalities • Inflammatory arthritis • Previous surgery **CLINICAL FEATURES** • Pain at first MTP joint • Restricted dorsiflexion • Difficulty during push off • Dorsal osteophyte • Swelling • Stiffness • Altered gait • Shoe discomfort **COMPLICATIONS** • Progressive joint degeneration • Gait alteration • Transfer metatarsalgia • Callus formation • Reduced walking tolerance **RADIOLOGY** • Joint space narrowing • Dorsal osteophytes • Subchondral sclerosis • Cystic changes • Advanced joint destruction **CONSERVATIVE MANAGEMENT** • Activity modification • NSAIDs/analgesics • Stiff soled shoes • Rocker bottom footwear • Orthoses • Physiotherapy • ROM exercises within tolerance • Intra articular injection in selected cases **SURGICAL MANAGEMENT** • Cheilectomy for selected early disease • Osteotomy in selected patients • First MTP arthrodesis for advanced disease • Joint replacement in selected patients --- **13 HALLUX VALGUS** **DEFINITION** Hallux valgus is a progressive deformity in which the great toe deviates laterally toward the lesser toes with medial deviation of the first metatarsal producing a prominent medial eminence or bunion **CAUSES/RISK FACTORS** • Genetic predisposition • Footwear with narrow toe box • Ligamentous laxity • Pes planus • Abnormal first ray mechanics • Rheumatoid arthritis • Neuromuscular disorders **CLINICAL FEATURES** • Lateral deviation of great toe • Medial bunion • Pain • Redness and pressure over bunion • Difficulty wearing shoes • Callosity • Reduced first MTP movement • Transfer metatarsalgia • Lesser toe deformity **COMPLICATIONS** • Bursitis • Osteoarthritis • Metatarsalgia • Hammer/claw toes • Skin ulceration • Persistent gait disturbance **RADIOLOGICAL FEATURES** Weight bearing X ray may show • Increased hallux valgus angle • Increased intermetatarsal angle • Medial deviation of first metatarsal • Subluxation of first MTP joint • Degenerative changes **CONSERVATIVE MANAGEMENT** • Wide toe box footwear • Avoid tight/high heeled shoes • Bunion pads • Orthoses • Toe spacers in selected cases • Analgesics • Physiotherapy • Calf/foot stretching and strengthening • Activity modification **SURGICAL MANAGEMENT** Indicated for persistent pain and functional limitation despite conservative treatment • Distal metatarsal osteotomy • Proximal metatarsal osteotomy • Lapidus procedure in selected patients • Akin osteotomy • First MTP arthrodesis for severe arthritis/deformity • Bunion correction according to deformity severity --- **14 HAMMER TOE** **DEFINITION** Hammer toe is a deformity of a lesser toe characterized by flexion deformity of the proximal interphalangeal PIP joint often with relative extension at the MTP and DIP joints **CAUSES** • Tight footwear • Muscle imbalance • Hallux valgus • Pes cavus • Rheumatoid arthritis • Neurological disorders • Tendon imbalance • Trauma **CLINICAL FEATURES** • Flexed PIP joint • Pain over prominent PIP joint • Corn formation • Callosity • Difficulty wearing shoes • MTP pain • Reduced toe mobility • Gait difficulty in severe cases **COMPLICATIONS** • Corns • Ulceration • Infection • MTP instability • Metatarsalgia • Fixed deformity • Difficulty walking **CONSERVATIVE MANAGEMENT** • Wide toe box shoes • Avoid tight footwear • Toe pads • Orthoses • Splinting/taping in flexible deformity • Toe stretching • Intrinsic muscle strengthening • Callus care **SURGICAL MANAGEMENT** For painful fixed deformity • Tendon release/transfer • PIP joint arthroplasty • PIP arthrodesis • MTP procedures when required • Correction of associated hallux valgus/pes cavus --- **15 METATARSALGIA** **DEFINITION** Metatarsalgia is a clinical condition characterized by pain and tenderness beneath the metatarsal heads usually caused by excessive or abnormal loading of the forefoot **CAUSES** • Excessive activity/running • Improper footwear • High heels Pes cavus Pes planus Hallux valgus Claw/hammer toes Short or long metatarsals Fat pad atrophy Stress fracture Inflammatory arthritis **CLINICAL FEATURES** Pain beneath metatarsal heads Burning or aching sensation Pain worsens during walking/running Forefoot tenderness Callosity Feeling of walking on a pebble Reduced walking tolerance **COMPLICATIONS** Chronic pain Altered gait Callus formation Lesser toe deformity Reduced physical activity Secondary knee/hip biomechanical problems **INVESTIGATIONS** Clinical examination Weight bearing X ray Ultrasound/MRI when neuroma stress fracture or soft tissue pathology is suspected **CONSERVATIVE MANAGEMENT** Activity modification Rest during painful period Proper footwear with wide toe box Metatarsal pads Custom orthoses Shock absorbing insoles Calf and foot stretching Foot intrinsic strengthening Weight management Analgesics/NSAIDs when appropriate **SURGICAL MANAGEMENT** Surgery is considered when persistent symptoms fail conservative treatment or when a structural cause is identified Metatarsal osteotomy Correction of associated hallux valgus Correction of lesser toe deformity Excision of neuroma where appropriate","thinking":"**Prioritizing Tool Usage**\n\nI'm considering the best way to approach problem-solving using available tools. Specifically, I'm now focused on tool selection. My current thinking prioritizes using the most specific tool for a given task, such as a file viewer