urns\r\n4. Diabetes mellitus\r\n5. Pressure and tissue ischemia\r\n6. Frostbite\r\n\r\nTypes of Gangrene\r\n\r\n1. Dry gangrene\r\n\r\n* Usually caused by progressive loss of arterial blood supply.\r\n* Tissue becomes dry, shriveled and black.\r\n* Usually develops slowly.\r\n* There is a clear line of demarcation between healthy and dead tissue.\r\n* Infection is initially minimal.\r\n\r\n2. Wet gangrene\r\n\r\n* Occurs when ischemic tissue becomes infected.\r\n* Tissue is swollen, soft and discolored.\r\n* Pus and foul-smelling discharge may be present.\r\n* Spreads rapidly.\r\n* Can lead to sepsi\n<truncated 2504 bytes>\n3. I am giving you one text in which there is written BPT University exam notes and page number. You have to remove it all, and in the ending also, because I am copying from PDF, so it is coming around in the text. \r\n\r\nSo, are you ready? Can I give you the text?\n4. PART I — SHOULDER Regional Conditions of the Shoulder — 20-Mark University Exam Answer Introduction The shoulder is a highly mobile joint complex consisting mainly of the glenohumeral, acromioclavicular, sternoclavicular and scapulothoracic articulations. Because of its wide range of movement and dependence on soft tissues for stability, it is commonly affected by painful disorders involving the joint capsule, rotator cuff tendons, biceps tendon and subacromial bursa. The important regional shoulder conditions include: 1. Periarthritis shoulder / Adhesive capsulitis 2. Rotator cuff tendinitis 3. Supraspinatus tendinitis 4. Infraspinatus tendinitis 5. Bicipital tendinitis 6. Subacromial bursitis 1. Periarthritis Shoulder / Adhesive Capsulitis Definition Adhesive capsulitis is a painful condition of the shoulder characterized by progressive pain, stiffness and marked restriction of both active and passive glenohumeral movements, particularly external rotation. It is commonly called frozen shoulder. Clini\n<truncated 36289 bytes>\n5. In this ear, to remove these lines with page number --- BPT / Orthopaedics — University Exam Answers Page 1 of 20                                                                                          here is the text ; ---------- 1. ACQUIRED TORTICOLLIS DEFINITION Acquired torticollis is a condition in which the head assumes an abnormal position due to shortening, spasm, pain or dysfunction of the cervical muscles, producing lateral flexion of the head to one side with rotation of the chin toward the opposite side. CAUSES • • • • • • • • Muscular causes ◦ ◦ ◦ Sternocleidomastoid spasm Cervical muscle strain Myofascial pain Trauma ◦ ◦ Whiplash injury Cervical fracture/dislocation Infection ◦ ◦ Retropharyngeal abscess Cervical tuberculosis Neurological causes ◦ ◦ Dystonia Cerebral lesions Ocular causes ◦ Abnormal ocular alignment Cervical spine disorders ◦ ◦ Cervical spondylosis Disc prolapse Inflammatory conditions ◦ Rheumatoid arthritis Postural causes CLINICA\n<truncated 20593 bytes>\n6. 1. Congenital Talipes Equinovarus (CTEV / Clubfoot)\r\nIntroduction & Definition\r\nCTEV is a complex congenital deformity of the foot characterized by four distinct components: Equinus (plantarflexion at the ankle), Varus (inversion of the subtalar joint), Adduction (of the forefoot), and Cavus (exaggerated medial longitudinal arch). It is commonly remembered by the mnemonic \"CAVE\".\r\n\r\nClinical Features\r\nThe foot is turned downwards and inwards, resembling a club.\r\nDeep creases are visible on the medial border and posterior aspect of the heel.\r\nThe heel is small and empty (calcaneus is pulled up).\r\nThe calf muscles (especially on the affected side) are visibly atrophied and underdeveloped.\r\nIt is a rigid deformity that cannot be passively corrected to a neutral position.\r\nCan be unilateral or bilateral.\r\nInvestigations\r\nClinical Diagnosis: Diagnosis is primarily clinical immediately at birth.\r\nX-rays (AP and Lateral views of the foot in stress dorsiflexion): Used to assess the severity and document the 